Let $G$ be the following signed digraph, where green arcs are positive and red arcs are negative (this convention is used throughout the paper): \[  egin{tikzpicture} \useasboundingbox (-0.9,-0.4) rectangle (2.2,0.7); 
ode (1) at (0,0){$1$}; 
ode (2) at (1,0){$2$}; 
ode (3) at (2,0){$3$}; \draw[thick,->,red] (1.150) .. controls (-1,+1) and (-1,-1) .. (1.210); \path[thick,->] (1) edge[Green] (2) (2) edge[red] (3) (3) edge[Green,bend right=50] (1) ; \end{tikzpicture} \] It is simple and has exactly two cycles, both negative. Let $f$ be the and-net on $G$, that is: $f_1(x)=\overline{x_1}\land x_3$ and $f_2(x)=x_1$ and $f_3(x)=\overline{x_2}$ for all configurations $x$ on $[3]$. We have the following tables: \[  egin{array}{c|c} x & f(x)\\hline 000 & 001\ 001 & 101\ 010 & 000\ 011 & 100\ 100 & 011\ 101 & 011\ 110 & 010\ 111 & 010\ \end{array} \quad  egin{array}{c|c} x & f^1(x)\\hline 000 & 000\ 001 & 101\ 010 & 010\ 011 & 111\ 100 & 000\ 101 & 001\ 110 & 010\ 111 & 011\ \end{array} \quad  egin{array}{c|c} x & f^2(x)\\hline 000 & 000\ 001 & 001\ 010 & 000\ 011 & 001\ 100 & 110\ 101 & 111\ 110 & 110\ 111 & 111\ \end{array} \quad  egin{array}{c|c} x & f^3(x)\\hline 000 & 001\ 001 & 001\ 010 & 010\ 011 & 010\ 100 & 101\ 101 & 101\ 110 & 110\ 111 & 110\ \end{array} \] The state diagram of the asynchronous automaton of $f$ is the following: \[  egin{array}{c}  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (000) at (0,0){{\em 000}}; 
ode (001) at (1.5,1.5){{\em 001}}; 
ode (010) at (0,3){{\em 010}}; 
ode (011) at (1.5,4.5){{\em 011}}; 
ode (100) at (3,0){{\em 100}}; 
ode (101) at (4.5,1.5){{\em 101}}; 
ode (110) at (3,3){{\em 110}}; 
ode (111) at (4.5,4.5){{\em 111}}; \draw[->,thick](000) .. node[outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1,2}} controls ++(-0.5,-1) and ++(0.5,-1) .. (000); \draw[->,thick](001) .. node[outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 2,3}} controls ++(-0.5,-1) and ++(0.5,-1) .. (001); \draw[->,thick](010) .. node[outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1,3}} controls ++(0.5,1) and ++(-0.5,1) .. (010); \draw[->,thick](101) .. node[outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 3}} controls ++(-0.5,-1) and ++(0.5,-1) .. (101); \draw[->,thick](110) .. node[outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 2,3}} controls ++(0.5,1) and ++(-0.5,1) .. (110); \draw[->,thick](111) .. node[outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 2}} controls ++(0.5,1) and ++(-0.5,1) .. (111); \path[thick,->,draw,black] (000) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 3}} (001) (001) edge[bend right=10] node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1}} (101) (010) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny 2} (000) (011) edge[bend right=10] node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1}} (111) (011) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 2}} (001) (011) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 3}} (010) (100) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1}} (000) (100) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 2}} (110) (100) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 3}} (101) (101) edge[bend right=10] node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1}} (001) (101) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 2}} (111) (110) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1}} (010) (111) edge[bend right=10] node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 1}} (011) (111) edge node[near start,outer sep=0,inner sep=0.5,fill=white,circle]{	iny {\em 3}} (110) ; \end{tikzpicture} \end{array} \] $f$ is synchronizing, since $w=231123$ is a synchronizing word for $f$. Indeed, $f^w(x)=001$ for all configurations $x$ on $[3]$, as shown below: \[  egin{array}{c} 000 \ 001 \ 010 \ 011 \ 100 \ 101 \ 110 \ 111 \ \end{array} \xrightarrow{f^2}  egin{array}{c} 000 \ 010 \ 110 \ 111 \ \end{array} \xrightarrow{f^3}  egin{array}{c} 001 \ 110 \ \end{array} \xrightarrow{f^1}  egin{array}{c} 101 \ 010 \ \end{array} \xrightarrow{f^1}  egin{array}{c} 001 \ 010 \ \end{array} \xrightarrow{f^2}  egin{array}{c} 001 \ 000 \ \end{array} \xrightarrow{f^3}  egin{array}{c} 001 \ \end{array} \] Let $h$ be the or-net on $G$, that is: $h_1(x)=\overline{x_1}\lor x_3$ and $h_2(x)=x_1$ and $h_3(x)=\overline{x_2}$ for all configurations $x$ on $[3]$. $h$ is synchronizing, since one can check that $w=231123$ is also a synchronizing word for $h$: we have $h^w(x)=110$ for all configurations $x$ on $[3]$. Since there are only two BNs on $G$ (namely $f$ and $h$), $w=231123$ is a synchronizing word for every BN on $G$.

Let $G$ and $H$ be the following signed digraphs: \[ G\quad  egin{array}{c}  egin{tikzpicture} \useasboundingbox (-0.9,-0.4) rectangle (2.2,0.7); 
ode (1) at (0,0){$1$}; 
ode (2) at (1,0){$2$}; 
ode (3) at (2,0){$3$}; \draw[thick,->,red] (1.150) .. controls (-1,+1) and (-1,-1) .. (1.210); \path[thick,->] (1) edge[Green] (2) (2) edge[red] (3) (3) edge[Green,bend right=50] (1) ; \end{tikzpicture} \end{array} \qquad H\quad  egin{array}{c}  egin{tikzpicture} \useasboundingbox (-0.9,-0.4) rectangle (2.2,0.7); 
ode (1) at (0,0){$1$}; 
ode (2) at (1,0){$2$}; 
ode (3) at (2,0){$3$}; \draw[thick,->,red] (1.150) .. controls (-1,+1) and (-1,-1) .. (1.210); \path[thick,->] (1) edge[red] (2) (2) edge[red] (3) (3) edge[red,bend right=50] (1) ; \end{tikzpicture} \end{array} \] Then $H$ is the $\{2,3\}$-switch of $G$. We deduce from the first point of Proposition~ef{pro:BN_switch} that every BN on $H$ is the $I$-switch of a BN on $G$. As shown in Example~ef{ex1}, $w=231123$ is a synchronizing word for every BN on $G$, and we deduce from the third point of Proposition~ef{pro:BN_switch} that $w$ is also a synchronizing word for every BN on $H$.

Let $H$ be the following signed digraph, which is strong, has no positive cycles and which is not a cycle: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (1) at (0,0){$1$}; 
ode (2) at (1,0){$2$}; 
ode (3) at (2,0){$3$}; \draw[thick,->,red] (1.150) .. controls (-1,+1) and (-1,-1) .. (1.210); \draw[thick,->,white] (3.30) .. controls (3,+1) and (3,-1) .. (3.-30); \path[thick,->] (1) edge[red] (2) (2) edge[red] (3) (3) edge[red,bend right=50] (1) ; \end{tikzpicture} \] By the second case of Theorem~ef{thm:main}, $H$ is and-or-synchronizing (see Example~ef{ex2}). Let $G$ be the following signed digraph, which has no positive cycles, no sources, and no initial cycles: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (1) at (0,0){$6$}; 
ode (2) at (1,0){$4$}; 
ode (3) at (2,0){$5$}; \draw[thick,->,red] (1.150) .. controls (-1,+1) and (-1,-1) .. (1.210); \draw[thick,->,white] (3.30) .. controls (3,+1) and (3,-1) .. (3.-30); \path[thick,->] (1) edge[red] (2) (2) edge[red] (3) (3) edge[red,bend left=50] (1) ; 
ode (1') at (0,1.5){$1$}; 
ode (2') at (1,1.5){$2$}; 
ode (3') at (2,1.5){$3$}; \draw[thick,->,red] (1'.150) .. controls (-1,+2.5) and (-1,0.5) .. (1'.210); \path[thick,->] (1') edge[red] (2') (2') edge[red] (3') (3') edge[red,bend right=50] (1') ; \path[thick,->] (1') edge[Green, bend right=13] (2) (2') edge[Green] (2) (3') edge[Green,bend left=13] (2) ; \end{tikzpicture} \] The two strong components of $G$ are and-or-synchronizing, since there are isomorphic to $H$. However, $G$ is not and-or-synchronizing. Indeed, let $f$ be the and-or-net such that all components of $f$ are conjunctions, excepted $f_6$ which is a disjunction. Let $X$ be the set of configurations $x$ on $[6]$ with $x_i=0$ for some $i\in [3]$, $x_4=0$ and $x_5=1$. One can easily check that $f^i(X)\subseteq X$ for every $i\in [6]$. Furthermore, given $x\in X$, since $x_5=1$ we have $f_6(x)=\overline{x_5}\lor\overline{x_6}=\overline{x_6}$. Consequently, given $x,y\in X$ with $x_6
eq y_6$, we have $f^i(x),f^i(y)\in X$ and $f^i_6(x)
eq f^i_6(y)$ for every $i\in [6]$, and we deduce that $f^w_6(x)
eq f^w_6(y)$ for any word $w$. Hence $f$ is not synchronizing.

The argument is roughly the following. If $x_i=y_i=0$ there is nothing to prove: we can take $w=\epsilon$. So suppose, without loss of generality, that $x_i=1$. The key points can be explained by assuming that $i$ has exactly two in-neighbors, say $j$ and $\ell$, both positive and distinct from~$i$. An easy application of Lemma~ef{lem:no_fixed_vertex_2} shows that there is a word $u$, not containing $i$, such that $f^u(x)$ is an $i$-unstable configuration in $G$. Hence, setting $x'=f^u(x)$ and $y'=f^u(y)$ we have the following situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x'_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x'_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x'_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y'_j=?$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y'_\ell=?$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y'_i=?$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \] Hence $f_i(x')=0$ and if $f_i(y')=0$ then we are done with $w=v,i$. So suppose that $f_i(y')=1$. Since $f_i$ is a conjunction, this implies $y'_j=y'_\ell=1$, so we have the following situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x'_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x'_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x'_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y'_j=1$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y'_\ell=1$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y'_i=?$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \] Setting $x''=f^i(x')$ and $y''=f^i(y')$ we obtain a clear situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x''_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x''_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x''_i=0$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y''_j=1$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y''_\ell=1$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y''_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \] By Lemma~ef{lem:no_fixed_vertex_2}, there is a shortest word $v$ such that $f^v(y'')_i=0$. Since $y''_i=1$, $v$ has a shortest prefix $	ilde v$ whose last letter is $j$ or $\ell$. Suppose that this is $\ell$. Then $i$ and $j$ do not appear in $	ilde v$ and thus, setting $x'''=f^{	ilde v}(x'')$ and $y'''=f^{	ilde v}(y'')$, we obtain the following situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x'''_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x'''_\ell=?$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x'''_i=0$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y'''_j=1$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y'''_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y'''_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \] Since $x'''_j=y'''_\ell=0$ we have $f_i(x''')=f_i(y''')=0$, thus $w=u,i,	ilde v,i$ has the desired properties. Furthermore, from the bound given in Lemma~ef{lem:no_fixed_vertex_2}, we obtain $|u|,|v|<F(n+2)-1$ so that $|w|<2F(n+2)$. The case where $i$ has more than two in-neighbors, with possibly some negative, is very similar. To treat the case where $i$ has a loop or is of in-degree one, some additional easy arguments have to be given.

An easy application of Lemma~ef{lem:no_fixed_vertex_2} shows that there is a word $u$, not containing $i$, such that $f^u(x)$ is an $i$-unstable configuration in $G$. Hence, setting $x'=f^u(x)$ and $y'=f^u(y)$ we have the following situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x'_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x'_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x'_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y'_j=?$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y'_\ell=?$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y'_i=?$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \]

Hence $f_i(x')=0$ and if $f_i(y')=0$ then we are done with $w=v,i$. So suppose that $f_i(y')=1$. Since $f_i$ is a conjunction, this implies $y'_j=y'_\ell=1$, so we have the following situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x'_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x'_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x'_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y'_j=1$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y'_\ell=1$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y'_i=?$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \]

Setting $x''=f^i(x')$ and $y''=f^i(y')$ we obtain a clear situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x''_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x''_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x''_i=0$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y''_j=1$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y''_\ell=1$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y''_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \]

By Lemma~ef{lem:no_fixed_vertex_2}, there is a shortest word $v$ such that $f^v(y'')_i=0$. Since $y''_i=1$, $v$ has a shortest prefix $	ilde v$ whose last letter is $j$ or $\ell$. Suppose that this is $\ell$. Then $i$ and $j$ do not appear in $	ilde v$ and thus, setting $x'''=f^{	ilde v}(x'')$ and $y'''=f^{	ilde v}(y'')$, we obtain the following situation: \[  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$x'''_j=0$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$x'''_\ell=?$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$x'''_i=0$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \qquad  egin{tikzpicture} 
ode (j) at (0,1){$y'''_j=1$}; 
ode (l) at (0,0){$y'''_\ell=0$}; 
ode (i) at (2,0.5){$y'''_i=1$}; \path[thick,->,Green] (j) edge (i) (l) edge (i) ; \end{tikzpicture} \]
